Usages:
  peer_transport_modes: |
    Each remote peer is reached through two independent mechanisms multiplexed underneath the
    Transporter facade: a long-lived stream (for the steady flow of normal raft messages, one
    stream per message type) and a pipeline (a pool of short-lived HTTP requests, used for
    larger or less latency-sensitive messages such as snapshots). Callers of Send/SendSnapshot
    never choose between them explicitly — the transport picks based on message type/size.

Annotations: |
  This cell is the network transport layer that ships raft messages — including full state
  snapshots — between cluster peers over HTTP. It sits directly below the raft consensus loop
  and above the OS network/HTTP stack: outbound messages are handed to it via the entity
  methods below, and inbound HTTP requests are decoded and handed back to the consensus layer
  through the `Raft` callback interface. It receives peer addresses as plain parameters to its
  own methods rather than importing the membership cell directly, keeping it decoupled from how
  the caller discovers cluster topology.

  Use `peer_transport_modes` when reasoning about latency/ordering guarantees for a given
  message type.

---

"Transporter()":
  location: transport.go
  annotations: |
    Facade over per-peer connections; the raft consensus loop's only view of the network.
  methods:
    "Start() -> err:error": |
      Begin serving inbound raft HTTP traffic and start background peer-probing.
    "Handler() -> handler:HTTPHandler": |
      Return the HTTP handler to mount for inbound raft traffic (pipeline, stream, and snapshot
      endpoints, plus a liveness-probing endpoint).
    "Send(messages []Message)": |
      Enqueue outbound raft messages for delivery to their target peers, chosen per
      `peer_transport_modes`.
    "SendSnapshot(snapshot SnapshotMessage)": |
      Send a full state snapshot to one peer, reporting completion/failure back through the
      `Raft` callback interface.
    "AddPeer(id uint64, urls []string)": |
      Begin tracking a new peer reachable at `urls`, starting its stream/pipeline machinery.
    "RemovePeer(id uint64)": |
      Stop tracking a peer and tear down its connections.
    "UpdatePeer(id uint64, urls []string)": |
      Update the URLs used to reach an existing peer, e.g. after its address changes.
    "ActiveSince(id uint64) -> since:Time": |
      Return when the connection to `id` most recently became active, or the zero time if
      never active.
    "ActivePeers() -> count:int": |
      Number of peers with at least one currently active connection.
    "Stop()": |
      Shut down all peer connections and stop serving inbound traffic.

"Raft()":
  location: transport.go
  annotations: |
    The callback contract the transport uses to hand inbound state back to the raft consensus
    state machine; implemented by the consensus loop, not by this cell.
  methods:
    "Process(message Message) -> err:error": |
      Deliver one inbound raft message to the consensus state machine for processing.
    "IsIDRemoved(id uint64) -> removed:bool": |
      Ask the consensus layer whether `id` has been removed from the cluster, so the transport
      can reject/ignore traffic from stale peers.
    "ReportUnreachable(id uint64)": |
      Notify the consensus layer that peer `id` is currently unreachable.
    "ReportSnapshot(id uint64, status SnapshotDeliveryStatus)": |
      Notify the consensus layer of the outcome of a previously sent snapshot.

